# Queue sweep

**This is report-only: a run changes no labels, issues, comments or files in the repo.
It reports gaps to the owner and the coordinator, who decide what to queue (SDLC-023), including check 5's own gap: a ticket that's neither handed out nor queued.**

Run the sweep in a mechanical-tier sub-agent, whatever tier the calling thread runs on.
The parent thread reads only the returned report.
This skill is the authorisation for spawning that sub-agent (SDLC-010).

This repo's pipeline (`.forge/feature-pipeline.md`, `post-merge-review` and the triage labels) relies on every stage writing a record the next one points at: a triage comment, a run log row, a checklist tick, a label.
Each of those is a small, separate action, so one can be skipped without anything downstream failing loudly.
A stage 8 Strong finding is called out in a comment but never turned into a ticket; a post-merge review Must survives in an archive comment with no sub-issue; a feature's checklist item is ticked by hand ahead of the state it describes; an issue sits with no label at all.
This skill is the sweep that catches that class of gap; it never fixes anything itself.

## Cadence

Run it as the first step of cutting a release, before the release notes are drafted for real (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, "Releases").
The Main Health thread also runs it on a standing schedule: see `.agents/skills/watch-main-ci/SKILL.md`'s Routine 1 for the cadence (the first daily firing of the existing trigger) and its "Per-repo values" table for where reports land.

## Report format

Write the report to `queue-sweep/<YYYY-MM-DD>-<shortsha>.md` in the working folder ({{ working_folder }}), using the date and `origin/main`'s short SHA at run time.
Post it in the running thread, and send it to the coordinator where the harness provides a way to message the coordinator.
**When run pre-release**, also post the report as a comment on the release ticket (SDLC-101): that's the durable record of the sweep outcome and the owner's per-gap decisions, not the working-folder copy.
**A standing run** (the Main Health thread's own schedule) stays in the working folder only: the daily standing sweep is not a scheduled assurance pass for SDLC-103 purposes, so it gets no hosting-platform record.

1. A header line: the UTC time and the owner's local time ({{ owner_timezone }}), the `origin/main` SHA this run read, and "nothing was changed."
2. One section per check below, **in order**, as a table: `#`, title, labels, gap, evidence (the query row or a comment link).
   A check with no gaps still gets its section, reading "none"; never omit a check because it found nothing.
3. **Not checkable this run**: each check (or part of a check) that couldn't run, and why, for example check 7 when the coordinator skill isn't on `origin/main`, or check 5 without the harness's thread-listing tools.
   Never report one of these as a drift finding.
4. **Suggested actions for the coordinator.**

## Query conventions

Queries are `gh api` REST calls, written in full with `per_page=100` and `--paginate` so a result isn't silently truncated to the platform's default page size.
Where a hosting-platform MCP tool is the only working access (some sandboxes run `gh` with no working token), use its equivalent listing or read call; the query shape stays the same.
A REST issue listing includes pull requests; every query below either filters `.pull_request` directly or says to drop rows carrying it.
`--paginate` applies `--jq` to each page separately, so a filter that reduces the whole result (`last`, `length`, `first`) gives one answer per page: stream elements with `.[] | ...` and reduce outside jq instead.
Where a proxy or token refuses the next-page links `--paginate` follows, page explicitly with `&page=N` on the `repos/...` path until a page comes back with fewer than 100 entries.

**A semantic search tool is not a literal one.**
An MCP issue-search tool may rank by meaning rather than match exact phrases, returning false positives for an exact `in:title` phrase or nothing for an exact `in:body` phrase that is provably present.
Any check below that uses an exact-phrase search needs its result cross-checked when it comes from such a tool: on a zero result or any doubt, fall back to paginating the plain issue listing and filtering titles and bodies for the literal substring in a script.

The shell variables below stand for this repo's values:

```
R=repos/{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}
PMR={{ post_merge_review_tracker or "<post_merge_review_tracker in .copier-answers.yml>" }}
IDEAS={{ deferred_ideas_epic or "<deferred_ideas_epic in .copier-answers.yml>" }}
HANGING={{ hanging_epic or "<hanging_epic in .copier-answers.yml>" }}
ARCH={{ arch_findings_epic or "<arch_findings_epic in .copier-answers.yml>" }}
LFS={{ local_full_suite_evidence_tracker or "<local_full_suite_evidence_tracker in .copier-answers.yml>" }}
```

"Ready label" means `ready-for-build`, `ready-for-structure` or `ready-for-human`. `ready-for-feature-design` is a state role, but never a queueable ready label: see check 5.

The operational safety rules are the ones `post-merge-review` already states (`.agents/skills/post-merge-review/SKILL.md`, "Operational safety rules").
This skill makes only read-only API calls, so most don't apply, but the no-paid-calls-without-an-explicit-ask rule still does if a check is ever dispatched to a sub-agent.

## The fourteen checks

### 1. Stage 8 Strong findings with no ticket

```
gh api -X GET search/issues --paginate -f per_page=100 \
  -f q='repo:{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }} is:issue in:title "Stage 8 architecture pass for"' \
  --jq '.items[] | {number, state, title}'
```

For each pass ticket that is **closed**:

- Find its feature issue: `gh api $R/issues/<n>/parent`.
- Read the feature's comments: `gh api --paginate "$R/issues/<feature>/comments?per_page=100"`.
- Take the comment whose body matches `^## Stage 8 architecture pass`.
- Within that comment, take the lines from the first line matching `^\*\*Strong` up to the next line matching `^\*\*`.
  Each `- ` bullet there is one Strong finding.

Report as gaps:

- (a) a closed pass ticket whose feature has no matching triage comment;
- (b) a Strong bullet with no issue reference;
- (c) a referenced issue that is open with no ready label (`gh api $R/issues/<N> --jq '[.labels[].name]'`);
- (d) any other issue the triage comment references that carries `needs-decision` (a Strong bullet's own referenced issue is (e) below, never this clause), that is open, is not labelled `hanging`, `future` or `deferred-arch-finding`, and is not among the feature's sub-issues (`gh api --paginate "$R/issues/<feature>/sub_issues?per_page=100"`): the pass must file these as tracker sub-issues (`.forge/feature-pipeline.md`, stage 8).
  A closed issue is fine; a parked one is fine only once a decision comment from the owner is on it (`.forge/feature-pipeline.md`, "A ticket is resolved when") — check with `gh api --paginate "$R/issues/<n>/comments?per_page=100" --jq '.[] | select(.author_association == "OWNER")'`, and treat no match as a gap: a ticket a thread parked and detached on its own, with no owner decision comment, is still a gap.
- (e) a Strong bullet's referenced issue that is open and labelled `hanging`, `future` or `deferred-arch-finding`: a Strong finding is fixed or escalated, never parked (owner decision, 2026-11-02), so this is a gap whatever decision comment is on it — (d)'s parking exemption, and its decision-comment check, apply only to a `needs-decision` ticket, never to a Strong finding's own issue.

A referenced issue that is closed is fine: it was actioned.

### 2. Post-merge review Musts and Should bundles with no ticket

Read the tracker's body: `gh api $R/issues/$PMR --jq .body`.
Parse the table under its `## Run log` heading by splitting each row on `|`.
Take the column positions from that table's own header row at run time (don't hardcode an index; the generated tracker starts with `Run | Window (merged) | PRs checked | Must | Should | Sub-issues | Archive`, but re-read the header each run), specifically Must, Should, Sub-issues and the Archive link.

Limit the rows to runs dated on or after the last release tag's date (`git log -1 --format=%cs $(git tag --sort=-v:refname | head -1)`).
When this check runs as the first step of a release, use every row since the tag before that one instead, so a run that already happened before the prior tag isn't re-flagged as new.
With no tags yet, use every row.

For each row with Must + Should > 0:

- Let `refs` be the distinct issue numbers mentioned in the Sub-issues column.
- The row is a gap if `refs` is empty, if `|refs| < Must` (not enough tickets for the Musts, which each need their own), or if `Should > 0` while `|refs| <= Must` (no separate Should bundle was ever filed; `post-merge-review` files Musts and Should bundles as separate tickets).
- For each issue in `refs`, it's a further gap if it is open with none of a ready label, `deferred-arch-finding`, or `needs-decision` (an out-of-scope or worth-exploring finding raised to the owner).
- For a flagged row, open its archive comment (`gh api $R/issues/comments/<id>`, from the row's Archive link) and quote the Must and Should headings it names as evidence, so the owner can see what went unfiled without re-deriving it.

Also check feature-scoped passes: `gh api -X GET search/issues --paginate -f per_page=100 -f q='repo:{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }} is:issue is:closed in:title "Post-merge review pass for"'`.
Apply the same refs test to each one's closing comment.

### 3. Feature-pipeline checklist drift and finished epics

```
gh api -X GET search/issues --paginate -f per_page=100 \
  -f q='repo:{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }} is:issue is:open in:body "## Feature pipeline"'
```

For each result:

- Parse lines matching `^- \[( |x)\] (\d)\. ` into item number and ticked state; keep any trailing annotation such as `(skipped — single-slice design)`.
- Get its sub-issues: `gh api --paginate "$R/issues/<n>/sub_issues?per_page=100"`.
- Split the sub-issues into pass tickets (titles starting `Post-merge review pass for` or `Stage 8 architecture pass for`) and build slices (everything else).

Report as a gap:

- item 6 ticked but no `Post-merge review pass for <feature>` ticket exists (check both the sub-issues and a repo-wide exact-title search, since `feature-pipeline` job (b) creates and links the ticket in two separate steps that can land out of step);
- item 6 ticked, no `Stage 8 architecture pass for <feature>` ticket exists, and item 9 carries no single-slice-skip annotation;
- item 7 unticked while every build slice is closed with `state_reason: completed`;
- item 8 unticked while the post-merge review pass ticket is closed;
- item 9 unticked while the stage 8 ticket and every Strong-finding and `needs-decision` ticket it filed are all resolved (closed, or a `needs-decision` ticket the owner parked, with an `author_association == "OWNER"` decision comment on it (check (d)'s command) and the ticket detached from the tracker so it no longer appears among the sub-issues);
- every item ticked and every sub-issue closed, but the feature issue itself is still open.

Separately, list open epics sitting at 100%:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=epic&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | select(.sub_issues_summary.percent_completed==100) | {number,title}'
```

Exclude the standing trackers (`.forge/tickets-and-branches.md`, which names them): `$PMR`, `$IDEAS`, `$HANGING`, `$ARCH`, `$LFS`, and any issue whose title starts `Post-merge review archive`.
Re-read that file at run time rather than trusting this list to stay current; it's sourced from there, not owned here.

### 4. Open issues with no state label

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | {number, title, labels: [.labels[].name]}'
```

State labels: `needs-triage`, `needs-info`, `needs-grilling`, `ready-for-build`, `ready-for-structure`, `ready-for-feature-design`, `ready-for-human`, `future`, `hanging`, and `deferred-arch-finding` (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`).
Exempt from this check: issues labelled `epic` (check 3 already covers those).
`release:*`, `bug`, `enhancement` and `breaking` are not state labels and don't satisfy this check on their own.

Report these lists:

- open issues with no state label at all;
- open issues carrying any label `docs/agents/triage-labels.md` lists as retired (expected to be empty; a hit means a migration missed an issue or something re-applied an old label).

### 5. Ready tickets not handed out and not in the queue

The ready set is open issues labelled `ready-for-build` or `ready-for-structure`:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=ready-for-build&state=open&per_page=100"
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=ready-for-structure&state=open&per_page=100"
```

`ready-for-feature-design` is never part of this ready set: a feature tracker carrying it routes to a one-off stage 3 design thread, never the queue, so it is never flagged here as "not queued" (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "`ready-for-feature-design`"). Instead, list every open `ready-for-feature-design` tracker with no stage 3 design comment yet, with its age, under its own "awaiting stage 3" heading:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=ready-for-feature-design&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | {number, title, created_at}'
```

For each result, check whether a stage 3 design comment already exists (a comment matching stage 3's own shape in `.forge/feature-pipeline.md` — facts verified against `origin/main`, the architecture, the decisions, the security section); a tracker with one is a labelling gap (stage 3 has started but didn't remove the label), reported separately from the "awaiting stage 3" list. A tracker genuinely awaiting stage 3 is not itself a gap; this is a listing, the same shape as check 9's `needs-decision`/`needs-grilling` rows, so a tracker can't sit unnoticed between stages 2 and 3.

Drop:

- blocked tickets: `gh api $R/issues/<n> --jq .issue_dependencies_summary.blocked_by` greater than 0, or a `Blocked by:` line in the body naming an issue that's still open;
- the pass tickets check 3 already found still blocked.

A remaining ticket counts as handed out if any of these holds:

- (a) it has an assignee;
- (b) an open PR references it: `gh api -X GET search/issues -f q='repo:{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }} is:pr is:open <n> in:body'`, confirmed by a `Ticket: #<n>` or `Closes #<n>` line in the PR body (not just the number appearing incidentally);
- (c) it carries the `in-flight` label;
- (d) a build-slot thread (named `Build <slot> <repo>`, per the coordinator core's "Naming") is working, blocked or ready for review, and its latest status line or message names the ticket; list threads and read each one's current status with the harness's thread tools, since a slot thread's title doesn't carry the issue number.

"In the coordinator's queue" means the ticket carries the `queued` label, or its number appears in the newest coordinator message in the project chat that lists queued tickets (read with the harness's chat-reading tool, newest first, until that message is found).

Report the tickets that are neither handed out nor queued (SDLC-023): a gap for the owner and the coordinator to decide, the same as every other check in this skill.
List it under "Suggested actions for the coordinator" as "queue now", since the coordinator's default-queue rule ("The label state machine") already says a ready ticket like this one should be queued without being asked, unless the ticket itself is `needs-decision`, `hanging`, `future`, `deferred-arch-finding`, carries an open `Blocked by:`, or the owner is on record holding it, in which case say so instead.

Also list the open review-pass tickets that lack `main-health`: a title starting `Post-merge review pass`, a title naming full-suite or CI-drift work, or a `Route: Main Health` line in the body, with no `main-health` label.
Expected to be empty.
A hit is a labelling gap, fixed by adding `main-health` (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "`main-health`").

**If this harness has no thread-listing or chat-reading tools** (part (d) and the chat read both need them), report under "Not checkable this run: queue not fully readable from this harness" instead of treating the residual list as gaps; don't count a ticket as un-queued just because this run couldn't see the queue.

### 6. Hanging children past their revisit date

The population is the union of:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=hanging&state=open&per_page=100"
gh api --paginate "$R/issues/$HANGING/sub_issues?per_page=100" --jq '.[] | select(.state=="open")'
```

Report a membership mismatch either way (labelled `hanging` but not a child of the hanging epic, or a child of the epic without the label) as its own list.

`docs/agents/triage-labels.md` documents `hanging`'s revisit trigger as the hanging epic's revisit cadence plus any revisit date or condition recorded in the issue itself.
This skill uses the one-line convention `Revisit: YYYY-MM-DD` (optionally `Revisit by:` or `Revisit on:`) for that per-issue date, and reads it with the pattern `(?im)^\**Revisit(?: by| on)?:?\**\s*(\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2})`, taking the last match in the body, then in the comments (newest first) if the body has none.

Two result lists:

- **past revisit date**: a `Revisit:` date found, and it is before today (UTC);
- **no revisit date recorded**: no `Revisit:` line found anywhere; show the issue's free-text trigger if one is obviously present in the body, otherwise just the issue number and title.
  Don't fall back to the epic's own blanket cadence as if it were a per-issue date: that would mark every child overdue at once and hide which ones are genuinely due.

If `docs/agents/triage-labels.md` ever tightens the revisit trigger to a specific format, this convention follows it.

### 7. Orchestration rules: coordinator skill vs. project settings

The coordinator reads its rules from `.forge/coordinator-core.md`, whose sections cite the source sections they condense. This check has three parts.

**(a) The core is whole and current with its sources.**

```
python3 scripts/check_coordinator_core.py shape
```

A non-zero exit is a gap: the core is over its size limit, missing its truncation guard, or missing its end marker. Quote the script's own report.
In TheForge's own repository only, also run TheForge's `lock` check, which compares each section's cited source sections with the root-only lock file; a consuming project has no lock and reports that part under "Not checkable this run". A failing `lock` check is a gap: a cited source section changed, or a citation broke, without the core being re-read and the lock refreshed.

**(b) The live project settings carry the current coordinator-core section.** Run this check only when this repository is the lead (the owner's `Lead repo:` line), or the only member; a non-lead member reports this part under "Not checkable this run: covered by the lead repository's sweep." Write the harness's project instructions as delivered in this running thread's own context verbatim to `settings.txt`, then:

```
python3 scripts/check_coordinator_core.py drift settings.txt
```

A non-zero exit is a gap, quoted from its report: the live text no longer contains the snippet (`docs/agents/coordinator-core-snippet.md`), or it exceeds the host's size limit. Report a member contradiction with no open `needs-decision` ticket for that member and section as a Should.

**(c) Session-start re-read, best effort.** Whether long-running threads have actually been told to re-read the current `CLAUDE.md`, skills and project instructions recently (`.forge/coordinator-core.md`, "Reuse, rotation and resolution") isn't something this check can verify directly; note it as a Could-only observation when a thread's own transcript suggests it hasn't happened in a long time, never as a finding on its own.

This check does not run, and is reported under "Not checkable this run" rather than as drift, in either of these cases:

- `.forge/coordinator-core.md` isn't on `origin/main` yet, or `scripts/check_coordinator_core.py` doesn't exist yet;
- this run's thread can't see the project instructions at all, in which case the report asks the coordinator to run this one check instead.

### 8. Process changes that cite no SDLC statement

Process changes are reviewed against `docs/software-development-lifecycle.md` (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, "Process changes follow the lifecycle doc"), and the PR's `Process:` line is how that review is recorded.
This check finds the ones that skipped it.

```
git fetch origin main
git log <last-sweep-sha>..origin/main --format='%H %s' -- CLAUDE.md .forge .agents/skills docs/agents
```

`<last-sweep-sha>` is the short SHA in the most recent report's file name under the report path; with no earlier report, use the commit that generated the repo.
Squash-merge subjects end in `(#<n>)`; fetch each PR's body (`gh api "$R/pulls/<n>" --jq .body`).
A PR is a candidate when its `Process:` line is absent, says `none` or `n/a`, or names statements the diff does not trace to.
Read its diff against the document's numbered statements.
Report it when the diff touches the subject of an `SDLC-` statement, or introduces or changes a policy with an engineering-management effect that no numbered statement justifies and directs, quoting the statement (or noting that none exists) and the hunk.
A process PR that does neither is not a gap.

Skip PRs that changed nothing but the lifecycle document itself; the owner's approval is its record.
Skip PRs whose only process change is a forge sync (`forge-sync`), whose record is the overrides log and the sync PR itself.
If the document isn't on `origin/main`, report the check under "Not checkable this run".

### 9. Open `needs-decision` and `needs-grilling` tickets

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=needs-decision&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | {number, title, created_at}'
```

This isn't a drift check like 1-8; it's a listing, because `needs-decision` (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "`needs-decision`") blocks a ticket regardless of its other labels and has no staleness threshold.
Report every result as its own row (`#`, title, labels, age in whole days since `created_at`, UTC, with the query itself as evidence) under its own "awaiting the owner" heading.
Feed that same list into progress reports under "anything awaiting the owner" (`.forge/coordinator-core.md`, "Progress reports", item 5).
An empty result still gets the heading, reading "none".

Run the same query and row shape for `needs-grilling` (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "`needs-grilling`"), excluding any result that also carries `needs-decision` — `needs-decision` and `needs-grilling` can legitimately stack (`docs/agents/triage-labels.md`, "`needs-decision`"), and a ticket carrying both is hard-blocked on the owner, not open for a grilling thread to pick up:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=needs-grilling&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | select([.labels[].name] | index("needs-decision") | not) | {number, title, created_at}'
```

Report it under its own "awaiting a grill" heading, same shape as the `needs-decision` list above — an empty result still gets the heading.
These tickets are not "awaiting the owner" and don't feed that progress-report item; they're work for a grilling thread, so feed them into progress reports under the queue-left section instead.
A `needs-grilling` ticket excluded here for also carrying `needs-decision` is not dropped: it already appears in the `needs-decision` listing above, and stays in the "awaiting the owner" group until the owner decides.

### 10. Queue empty only if nothing is queued and no feature epic is open with unowned work

The queue is empty only when both of these lists are empty.
Report each list as its own table with the query as evidence; an empty list reads "none".

**(a) Open tickets carrying a queue label.**

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=queued&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | {number, title, created_at}'
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=in-flight&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | {number, title, created_at}'
```

Report each row with its label.
An `in-flight` ticket whose PR has merged, or whose issue is closed, is a gap: the build thread did not drop its label.

**(b) Open feature epics with unowned open sub-issues.**
A sub-issue counts as owned when it carries `queued` or `in-flight`.
Get the open epics, dropping pull requests and the standing trackers check 3 excludes:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=epic&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.pull_request|not) | {number, title}'
```

For each epic that is a feature tracker, list its open sub-issues and keep the ones with neither queue label:

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues/<epic>/sub_issues?per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | select(.state=="open") | select([.labels[].name] | (index("queued") or index("in-flight")) | not) | {number, title}'
```

Each row is a gap: the coordinator must re-read the epic, file or label the sub-issue, and keep the queue open until it is closed.

Also report the outcome in one line: "queue empty" only when (a) and (b) are both none; otherwise, say which list is non-empty.

### 11. Scheduled pass overdue (SDLC-103)

A scheduled pass that leaves no record is read as a pass that didn't run (`.forge/process-and-evidence.md`, the scheduled-passes table).
This check reads the two passes whose own evidence is a hosting-platform record a query can check for staleness.
The others in that table need no overdue check: a skipped mutation sample or standing-sweep firing correctly leaves nothing, and the scheduled CI dispatch's own runs are already visible.

**(a) Post-merge review watermark.**

```
gh api "$R/issues/$PMR" --jq '{body}'
```

Parse the sha and merge timestamp from the body's `**Watermark:**` line, in its one canonical format (`post-merge-review` step 9): `<sha> (PR #<n>, merged <ISO8601>)`.
The PR number is part of that canonical line but this check has no use for it; nothing below reads it.
If the line still reads "none yet", the pass has never run: flag a gap only if any PR has merged at all.

Don't use the tracker's own `updated_at` to judge staleness: `post-merge-review`'s "Pending reviews" has the agent that merges a PR post a pending note on this same tracker as the merge's last step, so a comment lands on every merge whether or not a pass has run, and `updated_at` moves right along with it.
Read the comments themselves instead, which is the review's own evidence of whether it ran, not of whether anything merged.
Page explicitly rather than with `--paginate` (the same reason as "Query conventions" gives, and part (b) below): some proxies refuse the next-page links `--paginate` follows.

```
page=1
while :; do
  out=$(gh api "$R/issues/$PMR/comments?per_page=100&page=$page") || { echo API-FAILED; break; }
  printf '%s' "$out" | jq -c '.[] | select(.author_association == "OWNER" or .author_association == "MEMBER" or .author_association == "COLLABORATOR") |
    {created_at,
     pending_pr: ((.body | capture("<!-- pending-post-merge-review pr=(?<p>[0-9]+)")).p // null),
     covered: ((.body | capture("<!-- post-merge-review-archive covered=(?<c>[^ ]*) -->")).c // null)}'
  n=$(printf '%s' "$out" | jq 'if type == "array" then length else "x" end')
  case $n in ''|*[!0-9]*) echo API-FAILED; break ;; esac
  [ "$n" -lt 100 ] && break
  page=$((page + 1))
done
```

A marker from any other commenter does not count, pending or archive: on a public repository, anyone can post one, and trusting it would let an outside comment silently narrow or widen the review window, or forge a covered-PR claim.
A comment matching neither capture is ignored, including a documented note of a different, distinctly-marked shape such as `watch-main-ci/SKILL.md.jinja`'s Routine 2 missing-script note — it is neither a pending note nor an archive comment, and must never be mistaken for either.

Comments come back oldest first (the same ordering part (b) below relies on), so the lines this loop prints are oldest first too.
Each `covered` field is a comma-separated list of PR numbers (`post-merge-review`, step 7, posts this marker on every archive comment).
Union every line's `covered` list, split on commas, into one set.
Take the first line with a non-null `covered` as `<first-marked-archive>`, and the last such line as `<latest-marked-archive>` (either "none" if the tracker has no marked archive yet, e.g. every archive comment on it predates this marker).

A pending note (by its own `pending_pr`) counts as swept if either holds:

- its PR number appears in the unioned `covered` set; or
- `<first-marked-archive>` exists and the note's `created_at` is before it — grandfathering a note left over from before this marker existed, when every earlier archive comment was unmarked prose that this check could only tell apart from a pending note by elimination.

Once `<first-marked-archive>` exists, a pending note posted after it is swept only by appearing in some `covered` list, never by timestamp alone: this is what makes a partial run's deliberately-left-pending notes (`post-merge-review` step 1, "leave substantial newly merged PRs for the next pass") correctly stay unswept instead of being silently cleared by the next run's archive comment just because it landed later.
Before `<first-marked-archive>` exists at all (no marked archive comment has ever been posted), every pending note is swept by the old rule instead: unswept only if posted after the latest comment on the tracker of any kind, since there is nothing marked yet to check it against.

Flag a gap when either:

- the oldest unswept pending note is more than 3 days old; or
- (independent of whether any pending note exists at all) the oldest PR merged since `<cutoff>` is more than 3 days old:
  ```
  page=1
  : > /tmp/merged-since-cutoff.jsonl
  while :; do
    out=$(gh api "/search/issues?q=repo:{{ repo_owner }}/{{ repo_name }}+type:pr+is:merged+merged:>=<cutoff>&per_page=100&page=$page") || { echo API-FAILED; break; }
    printf '%s' "$out" | jq -c '.items[].pull_request.merged_at' >> /tmp/merged-since-cutoff.jsonl
    n=$(printf '%s' "$out" | jq '.items | length')
    case $n in ''|*[!0-9]*) echo API-FAILED; break ;; esac
    [ "$n" -lt 100 ] && break
    page=$((page + 1))
  done
  sort /tmp/merged-since-cutoff.jsonl | head -n1
  ```
  `<cutoff>` is the later of `<latest-marked-archive>`'s `created_at` (if any marked archive exists) and the watermark line's own `merged <ISO8601>` (if the line isn't "none yet") — the canonical watermark line already carries that timestamp, so this needs no separate lookup. Each is taken as the full timestamp, never truncated to a date.
  Use `<latest-marked-archive>`, never `<first-marked-archive>`, here: anchoring the backstop on the *first* marked archive ever posted would keep re-flagging the same early PRs as overdue forever, even long after a later run's `covered` list has actually handled them.
  Take the full result set's *earliest* `merged_at`, not the earliest-*created* matching PR's: the search API has no `sort=merged`, and a PR's creation order can diverge from its merge order enough to mask a real backlog behind a newer-looking timestamp, so page through every match and sort client-side instead of trusting `sort=created` to approximate it.
  Taking the watermark PR's own merge time, not only a marked archive's, is what keeps this backstop live on a tracker that has run before but whose archive comments all predate the marker: without it, a tracker with no marked archive yet and no pending notes left to check would report clean while merges pile up with zero evidence for them — the same inversion the pending-note check alone cannot catch, now surviving in the one case this check has run before but not since the marker existed.
  Skip the backstop only when neither source gives a `<cutoff>` (truly never run, which the watermark's own "none yet" case above already covers).
  This is the backstop for the case pending notes alone cannot catch: merges continuing while no new note lands at all, which would otherwise read as "no unswept note, so not overdue" — backwards from SDLC-103's own "absence of evidence means the pass didn't run."

No unswept note, no such merge, or neither older than 3 days, is not a gap, however old `updated_at` reads.

**(b) Local full-suite evidence tracker.**
Skip this part, reporting it under "Not checkable this run: no local full-suite script yet", while `scripts/local-full-suite.sh` is not on `origin/main`.

The canonical record is a comment on the local full-suite evidence tracker `$LFS`, never a commit status: some environments' GitHub proxy refuses commit-status writes outright, so a check that only read statuses would wrongly read those environments as permanently overdue even while the script runs cleanly.
A full run's comment carries a single-line marker `<!-- local-full-suite-evidence sha=<sha> finished=<ISO8601> aggregate=pass|fail -->`; a resumed or partial run posts no such comment, so its absence between two full runs is not itself a gap.
A marker from any other commenter does not count: on a public repository, anyone can post one, and trusting it would let an outside comment plant a false "this ran clean" record.

```
page=1
while :; do
  out=$(gh api "$R/issues/$LFS/comments?per_page=100&page=$page") || { echo API-FAILED; break; }
  printf '%s' "$out" | jq -c '.[] | select((.body | test("<!-- local-full-suite-evidence sha=")) and (.author_association == "OWNER" or .author_association == "MEMBER" or .author_association == "COLLABORATOR"))'
  n=$(printf '%s' "$out" | jq 'if type == "array" then length else "x" end')
  case $n in ''|*[!0-9]*) echo API-FAILED; break ;; esac
  [ "$n" -lt 100 ] && break
  page=$((page + 1))
done | tail -n1
```

Comments come back oldest first, so the last line is the newest marked comment.
The loop pages explicitly rather than with `--paginate`, for both reasons in "Query conventions".
A last line of `API-FAILED` means a call failed or returned something other than a list partway, so report this part "not checkable" rather than reading an earlier page's marker as the newest evidence.
Take its `created_at` only as a fallback — prefer the `finished=` value inside the marker itself, since the comment's own timestamp is when the API call happened, not necessarily when the run finished.
Parse `sha=`, `finished=` and `aggregate=` out of the marker line (pattern: `sha=(\S+) finished=(\S+) aggregate=(\S+)`); treat a comment where any of the three is missing as malformed, equivalent to no evidence for that run.
Compare `finished` to now, and `sha` to `origin/main`'s current head (`git rev-parse origin/main`, or `gh api "$R/commits/main" --jq .sha`).

Flag a gap when either:

- a marked comment was found, it is more than 24 hours old, **and** `main`'s head has moved since that comment's `sha` (a quiet period where `main` simply hasn't moved is not a gap, since there would be nothing new to re-verify); or
- no comment on the tracker carries the marker at all (not an API failure, so this isn't "not checkable": an evidence tracker with zero full-run evidence on it is itself the gap).

Where the environment also allows commit-status writes, a `local-full-suite` aggregate status will usually exist too on the same commit, but this check never depends on it and never reads it: the tracker comment alone is sufficient everywhere this routine runs, including an agent environment that cannot write commit statuses at all.

Report both (a) and (b) as their own rows, each reading "none" when clean.
A gap here is "not checkable this run" only when the API calls themselves fail (rate limit, no token); it is never silently skipped.

### 12. Stale coordinator boot receipt

The boot routine (`.forge/coordinator-core.md`, "Start-of-session self-check") has every coordinator session write a receipt carrying the `origin/main` sha it booted from, as the first line of its checklist and again appended to `{{ working_folder }}/coordinator/boot-log.md`. This check finds a receipt that has fallen behind a later change to either file the boot routine reads.

In a project with several members, the one coordinator writes this receipt to the lead's working folder, not each member's own: run this check only in the lead repository's sweep (or the only member's), the same gating as 7(b), and read `boot-log.md` from the lead's working folder (its `## Repo facts` row) rather than this repository's own.

```
tail -n1 {{ working_folder }}/coordinator/boot-log.md
```

Parse `<sha>` out of the line's `@ <sha>;` segment. If the file doesn't exist yet, or its last line doesn't match that shape, report "none yet" — not itself a gap: nothing has booted since this check (or the boot routine itself) existed.

```
git fetch origin main
git diff --name-only <sha> origin/main -- .forge/coordinator-core.md .agents/skills/build-thread-brief/SKILL.md
```

A non-empty result is a gap: the coordinator core file or the build-thread-brief skill changed on `origin/main` after the latest receipt's sha, so that receipt no longer reflects what the coordinator actually read. Flag it as a **Should**, never a Must: the next boot (the next compaction, or the coordinator's own next session) picks up the change on its own, so a receipt sitting stale for a while is a staleness gap, not an unsafe one.

**Worked example.** `boot-log.md`'s last line reads `Booted from lead-repo @ a1b2c3d; design/review=opus, build=sonnet, mechanical=haiku; timer armed: yes`. A later commit `f0e9d8c` edits `.forge/coordinator-core.md`'s "Slots and the queue" section. `git diff --name-only a1b2c3d origin/main -- .forge/coordinator-core.md .agents/skills/build-thread-brief/SKILL.md` returns `.forge/coordinator-core.md`: non-empty, so this is a gap — flag "boot receipt stale: coordinator core changed since `a1b2c3d`" as a Should. Had `f0e9d8c` instead touched only an unrelated file (say `docs/principles.md`), the same `git diff --name-only` would return nothing, and the receipt would still be current: no gap.

This check does not run, and is reported under "Not checkable this run" rather than as drift, when `.forge/coordinator-core.md`'s "## Start-of-session self-check" section isn't on `origin/main` yet, or when this run's thread can't read the project's shared files at all.

### 13. Post-merge review tracker body corrupted

The tracker's own body (`post-merge-review` step 9, "Keeping the tracker tidy") carries only the watermark, the procedure pointer and the run log index; a pending note or an archive record is always a comment, never a body edit (`post-merge-review`, "Pending reviews"). A stray body-write that sends a pending note's own text as the new body, rather than posting it as a comment, destroys the watermark and run log this check exists to protect, with nothing else noticing until the next pass is due.

```
gh api "$R/issues/$PMR" --jq '{body}'
```

Flag a gap when either:

- the body does not contain the literal text `**Watermark:**`; or
- the body contains a pending-note or archive marker (`<!-- pending-post-merge-review` or `<!-- post-merge-review-archive`) — markers that belong only in comments, so either one appearing in the body is itself the signature of this failure, not merely a missing-watermark side effect.

Either condition is a Must: the tracker is this repo's only durable record of the standing cadence's progress, and losing it silently is the exact "absence of evidence" failure SDLC-103 exists to catch, this time of the record itself rather than of whether a pass ran. Restore the body from the latest run log entry's archive comment (the last-known-good source), then raise, as its own `needs-decision` finding, how the body-write happened, so the write path that caused it gets fixed rather than only its symptom.

A gap here is "not checkable this run" only when the API call itself fails; it is never silently skipped.

### 14. `ready-for-structure` left on after its own design-exit pass is already done

A backstop for the mechanical label sync `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`'s "Mechanical label sync after design-type work" requires at `implement-with-merge` step 0a: this check catches a ticket where that step was skipped.

```
gh api --paginate "$R/issues?labels=ready-for-structure&state=open&per_page=100" \
  --jq '.[] | {number, title, body}'
```

For each result, flag it as a gap when its already-fetched body, or `gh api --paginate "$R/issues/<n>/comments" --jq '.[].body'`, contains a `Structure guidance` heading (the design-exit pass has already run and written it, per `implement-with-merge` step 0a, into either place).

That condition means the design-exit pass that was supposed to flip this ticket to `ready-for-build` has already happened; report it as a labelling gap, fixed by removing `ready-for-structure` and adding `ready-for-build` (never by re-running the design-exit pass).
A result with no `Structure guidance` heading is not a gap for this check: the ticket is still genuinely waiting on its design-exit pass.
A feature's own tracker never carries `ready-for-structure`, so it never appears in this check's result set; check 5's "awaiting stage 3" listing is the equivalent backstop for `ready-for-feature-design`.

## Not built, and why

- **No `docs/agents/` reference page.** It would fail the deletion test: it would only repeat this file.
  Every procedure in this family (`post-merge-review`, `watch-main-ci`) keeps its queries in the skill itself.
- **No script or runtime code.** Every check here is an agent-run API read.
  A script would add a coverage obligation and a parser for prose that the owner and threads write by hand, and an agent reads that prose better.
  If a dry run shows a check's parsing is unreliable by hand, file that as its own ticket rather than building a parser ad hoc.
- **No tracker issue of its own.** This skill reports and keeps no state (no watermark, because every run is a full sweep), and it never posts to the tracker except a pre-release report on the release ticket.
- **It never edits `docs/agents/triage-labels.md`.** It only reads the labels defined there.
